Z||000007||Eric||09-04-99||05:59 PM||||Wouldn't the piston to deck clearance be less if you use a dished 3.1 piston verses a stock flat top in the 3.1 rebuild and still retain a compression ratio of 9.0:1, even higher than stock, or does the extra stroke change the ratio enough so there is no considerable change in piston to deck clearance.  The reason I ask is that I just got my pistons from Summit and there seems to be a dish of about 3/32 of an inch.  I'm trying to figure out how this will raise my compression ratio.  The number of these Federal Mogul hypereutectic pistons is H562CP (the C is for coated).  They cost about $90 before shipping.  There are two types of pistons for the normally aspirated '90 Camaro, one has a deeper dish than the other and you must specify what kind you want.
Z||000008||Harry||09-04-99||06:07 PM||||Piston manufactor's use a variety of methods to change compression.<br>The dished piston has a face further from the deck surface of the block.<br>However, the bore for the piston pins can be moved either up or down to change stroke/compression ratio.
Z||000009||batboy||09-06-99||08:47 PM||||Hate to nitpick Harry, but changes in the piston pin location does not change the stroke. Changing the crank changes the stroke, but you must use the right pistons for that crank.<p>The amount of dish in the top of the piston does affect compression though. So can the size of the compression chamber in the head, thickness of the head gasket, and milling the head.<p>Also, if you are rebuilding the engine, put a cam in that is the lift you desire and forget about the 1.6 rockers. They change the valve train geometry slightly and might cause premature wear.
Z||000010||lowCG||09-06-99||10:38 PM||||Hey,Batboy,<br>can you do some simulations of the effects of different cam lifts on the stock 2.8 heads to see just how effective cam lift increases are on that motor(to what degreee of increase)?<br>With that Desktop Dyno of yours.<p>Dish top pistons are for diesels(I thought),and pin heights aren't really easily adjustable either. 
Z||000011||Eric||09-08-1999||02:52 AM||||If 1.6 rocker arms are too much, then wouldn't 1.55 ratio be a good comprimise?  With small block studs, I have the option of using these "oddball" parts previously reserved for V8s.  The whole operation is described in detail in the Chevy Power book.  The bigger studs strengthen the valve train too.||||reg||
Z||000012||Carrolles||09-14-1999||11:36 AM||||Stroker,<P>I live in Alabaster and shop at Ken's too.<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||09-15-1999||01:58 PM||||Eric, I talked to my machinist and he said that oversized pistons (used when you bore out the cylinders) are often slightly dished to compensate for the compression ratio increase caused by boring it out. Often people will have the block decked and the heads milled too which will further increase the compression. They do this so that the piston doesn't stick up past the deck when it's at top dead center (which is very bad). He also said that the advertised compression ratio for pistons is purely an average, not always exact, depending on a number of factors.<P>LowCG, I didn't mean to ignore you, just now saw your question to me. I'm not sure if I understand what you're asking me, but if you mean will the computer program calculate clearance between the valves and piston tops due to different cam lifts, then no, it will not do that. It will calculate power output differences for cam lift though.||||reg||1
